    //     light as far as it takes for the AIM to stand no more than OFF_LIGHT (38 degrees, the designer's number) from the light in space (capB: 30 halvings between the light's
    //     own bearing and the bearing asked; were even the light's own bearing outside the bound, it is the light's own bearing).
    //   ELEVATION: the gaze keeps the height at which it left the trunk (K_Y's last key, 40 m) and rests at that height over the clearing's far rim, taken as far off as the trunk and
    //     the clearing's radius beyond it. So at a knot where the lens is under that height it is a little up, and at a knot where the lens has climbed past it a little down: along the floor, NOT LEVEL. The designer took it so: "level was my word for calm, and a tip-down of 14.8 degrees a second is the worse thing."
    // The AIM is the blend src/core/engine.js makes on the swing with the camera this world returns: the look LOOK_AHEAD metres ahead along the course, then this gaze at its row's
    // weight GAZE_W (the row's bell is whole on the swing and no other look is in play there). THAT IS THE SWEEPING CAMERA'S AIM, and the bound is the sweeping camera's alone: with the engine's steady setting the look ahead is longer and every look's weight less, capB knows neither, and the bound is not kept there. The designer: "the bound does NOT bind under the steady setting, which follows the route and none of a world's looks". The light is where the dusk law has it at that place (lightEl): by day it stands
    // higher, so the bound in space holds the bearing nearer the light's own there than at the other hours.
    // Between two knots the smooth step can carry the aim a little past the bound, so gaze() holds the bound itself at every place of the swing (capB again).
    // The first three rows' bearings are half way to the trunk; at the last two the bound holds the bearing short of half way.
    const GAZE_W = 0.86, LOOK_AHEAD = 22, OFF_LIGHT = 38 * Math.PI / 180;   // the keyed gaze's weight in pois and the camera's look-ahead (both used in the world's return, below); the designer's bound
    const _g = new THREE.Vector3(), _ah = new THREE.Vector3(), _li = new THREE.Vector3();
